What Makes a Vehicle a Lemon in California?

California’s Lemon Law operates under the Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act. The law generally protects consumers who purchase or lease qualifying vehicles covered by a manufacturer’s warranty.

A vehicle may qualify as a lemon when a manufacturer cannot repair a substantial defect after receiving a reasonable opportunity to do so. The defect must affect the vehicle’s use, safety, or value in a meaningful way.

Several factors can influence whether a vehicle qualifies, including:

- The nature and severity of the defect

- The number of repair attempts

- The length of time spent at repair facilities

- The vehicle’s warranty status

- Communications with the manufacturer or dealership

- The impact of the defect on vehicle operation

The specific facts of each case determine whether legal protections apply.

Common Vehicle Defects That May Support a Claim

Some defects directly affect vehicle safety or reliability. Repeated failures can also create substantial inconvenience when repairs do not resolve the underlying problem.

Potentially significant defects may involve:

Engine and Transmission Problems

Engine stalling, excessive oil consumption, overheating, transmission slipping, and repeated power loss can interfere with normal driving. Multiple repair attempts may provide important evidence when these conditions remain unresolved.

Steering and Brake Defects

Problems involving steering, braking, or other essential control systems can create serious safety concerns. Documentation from repair facilities can help establish the nature of the issue and the manufacturer’s efforts to correct it.

Electrical and Technology Failures

Modern vehicles rely on numerous electronic systems. Persistent electrical failures, dashboard warnings, malfunctioning cameras, charging problems, and other electronic defects can affect vehicle operation.

Safety System Issues

Airbag, seatbelt, backup camera, sensor, and other safety-related problems may receive particular attention because they can affect the vehicle’s safe operation.

A cosmetic problem generally presents different legal considerations from a defect that prevents reliable transportation or creates a safety risk.

The California Lemon Law Presumption

California law includes a Lemon Law presumption that can help establish whether a manufacturer received a reasonable number of opportunities to repair a vehicle. Certain circumstances can strengthen a potential claim.

The presumption may apply when:

- A serious safety defect remains after two or more repair attempts.

- A substantial defect remains after four or more repair attempts.

- The vehicle has been unavailable for warranty repairs for more than 30 cumulative days.

These standards provide useful guidelines for evaluating a potential Lemon Law case. Meeting a presumption does not automatically determine the outcome. Other facts can still affect whether a vehicle qualifies for protection.

Repair history remains especially valuable during this evaluation.

Why Repair Documentation is Relevant

Repair records provide a timeline of the vehicle’s problems. They can show when a defect first appeared, how many repair visits occurred, and what work the dealership performed.

Useful documents can include:

- Repair orders and invoices

- Service department reports

- Warranty documents

- Purchase or lease agreements

- Vehicle registration records

- Manufacturer correspondence

- Emails or written communications

- Records showing extended periods without vehicle access

Descriptions on repair orders can also reveal whether the same problem returned after previous repairs. Clear documentation gives legal counsel a stronger foundation for evaluating the claim.

